# Package.json Format Standards

## Purpose and Scope
This rule establishes consistent package.json formatting, field ordering, and metadata standards to ensure 
maintainable, discoverable, and properly configured Node.js packages across all projects.

## Standards and Requirements

### 1. Required Core Fields
Every package.json must contain these fields in the specified order:
- `name` - Package name following npm naming conventions
- `version` - Semantic version (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH)
- `description` - Clear, concise package description
- `type` - Explicitly set to "module" or "commonjs"
- `main` - Entry point for CommonJS
- `module` - Entry point for ES modules (if applicable)
- `types` - TypeScript declaration file path (if applicable)

### 2. Field Ordering Standard
Fields must appear in this exact order:
1. Core identification (name, version, description)
2. Module system (type, main, module, types, exports)
3. Scripts and tooling (scripts, dependencies, devDependencies)
4. Repository and author info (repository, author, license)
5. Additional metadata (keywords, files, engines)

### 3. Script Naming Conventions
- Use lowercase with hyphens: `build-prod`, not `buildProd`
- Standard script names: `dev`, `build`, `test`, `lint`, `format`
- Prefix environment-specific scripts: `build:prod`, `test:unit`

### 4. Dependency Management
- Pin exact versions for tools and build dependencies in devDependencies
- Use semantic ranges for runtime dependencies only when appropriate
- Group related dependencies logically
- Remove unused dependencies regularly

### 5. Metadata Requirements
- `license` field must be SPDX-compliant
- `repository` must include type and URL
- `author` should include name and contact information
- `keywords` should be relevant and discoverable

## Examples

### Good Examples

```json
{
  "name": "@company/awesome-package",
  "version": "1.2.3",
  "description": "A lightweight utility library for data transformation",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "./dist/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/index.js",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs",
      "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
    }
  },
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "build": "tsc && vite build",
    "build:prod": "NODE_ENV=production npm run build",
    "test": "vitest",
    "test:unit": "vitest run --reporter=verbose",
    "lint": "eslint src --ext .ts,.tsx",
    "format": "prettier --write src"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "lodash": "^4.17.21",
    "axios": "^1.4.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": "5.1.6",
    "vite": "4.4.9",
    "vitest": "0.34.3",
    "eslint": "8.47.0",
    "prettier": "3.0.2"
  },
  "repository": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "https://github.com/company/awesome-package.git"
  },
  "author": "Jane Doe <jane@company.com>",
  "license": "MIT",
  "keywords": [
    "utility",
    "data",
    "transform",
    "typescript"
  ],
  "files": [
    "dist",
    "README.md",
    "LICENSE"
  ],
  "engines": {
    "node": ">=18.0.0",
    "npm": ">=9.0.0"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "name": "simple-app",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "description": "A simple React application",
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "build": "vite build",
    "preview": "vite preview",
    "test": "jest",
    "lint": "eslint src"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "react": "^18.2.0",
    "react-dom": "^18.2.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@vitejs/plugin-react": "4.0.4",
    "vite": "4.4.9",
    "eslint": "8.47.0"
  },
  "author": "John Smith <john@example.com>",
  "license": "MIT"
}
```

### Bad Examples

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "react": "^18.2.0"
  },
  "name": "my-app",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "react-scripts start"
  },
  "version": "1.0.0"
}
```
*Fails: wrong field ordering, missing required fields, no description*

```json
{
  "name": "MyAwesomePackage",
  "version": "v1.2.3",
  "description": "",
  "main": "index.js",
  "scripts": {
    "buildProd": "webpack --mode=production",
    "testUnit": "jest --config=jest.unit.config.js"
  },
  "license": "Custom License"
}
```
*Fails: PascalCase name, version prefix, empty description, camelCase scripts, non-SPDX license*

```json
{
  "name": "test-package",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "dependencies": {
    "lodash": "*",
    "express": ">=4.0.0",
    "axios": "1.4.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": "^5.1.6"
  }
}
```
*Fails: wildcard dependency, overly broad range, inconsistent versioning strategy*

## Implementation Guidelines

### Package Naming
- Use kebab-case for package names
- Include scope for organizational packages: `@company/package-name`
- Avoid trademark conflicts and reserved names
- Keep names descriptive but concise

### Version Management
- Follow semantic versioning strictly
- Use `npm version` commands to update versions
- Tag releases in version control
- Document breaking changes in major versions

### Script Organization
- Group related scripts with consistent prefixes
- Use `pre` and `post` hooks appropriately
- Avoid complex inline scripts - extract to separate files
- Document custom scripts in README

### Dependency Best Practices
- Audit dependencies regularly with `npm audit`
- Use exact versions for critical tools and build dependencies
- Prefer peer dependencies for plugins and extensions
- Remove unused dependencies during cleanup cycles

### File Inclusion
- Use `files` field to explicitly control published files
- Include only necessary files in published packages
- Use `.npmignore` for complex exclusion patterns
- Test published package contents with `npm pack`

### Engine Constraints
- Specify minimum Node.js version based on features used
- Include npm/yarn version constraints when needed
- Test against specified engine versions in CI
- Document runtime requirements in README

